> I don't think so. On average, a random room contains about 0.42
> objects. 10% of all objects are weapons, of which 5% are long
> swords. Makes about 0.2% chance of a random room containing a long
> sword, although shops might change that number somewhat.

Well, I never thought that the chances were so bad... :-) Will not
certain monsters have a long sword often? (Barrow wights? Soldiers? Mine
town guards?)

> On the other hand, there's a 1/60 = 1.67% chance for a random room to
> contain an altar ready to serve you for your artifact needs.

But you need more than an altar. You need monsters to sacrifice and you
need to survive them before getting your artifact... (In slashem even
converting the altar can kill you.) So you should multiply that
percentage at least by a figure describing the probability to get a wand
of create monster or several scrolls of create monster by the time you
have an altar ready. If that is lower than about 12%, the chance of
getting a long sword may be higher than getting an artifact by
sacrificing. Also the artifact you obtain might be one of the less
useful ones...

Of course, finding a long sword is not sufficient to get an artifact.
Dipping can have negative side effects, too. But you have a long sword
to fight the monsters that may arise (water mocassins mostly; if you get
a nymph and can't kill it fast, it is best to run; in the case of a
hostile water demon you should never attack it yourself, because it may
summon a demon lord...)

In any case, I have the feeling that as a lawful I often get Excalibur
before any other decent artifact. But that may have to do with the fact
that I am playing slash'em, where there are more guaranteed weapons.
(For example, the Rat King, if rats are created, will normally have a
long sword. At least, one is guaranteed on the level, which has a
probability of 50% of being created, and is not one of the most
difficult levels.)


